{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>According to all the pattern of the Tabernacle and all the pattern of its vessels.</b> It is known that the Tabernacle and its vessels are all physical representations from which to contemplate supernal representations, of which they are a likeness. Among the wondrous matters included in it is what you find: that the Tabernacle comprises three parts — within the veil, outside the veil which is the Tent of Meeting, and the court of the Tabernacle — corresponding to existence, which is divided into three parts: the world of angels, the world of the spheres, and the world of the lowly. So too we find that man, who is the essence of all, is a likeness of existence in its three parts, and is called a microcosm, and he is divided into three parts: the world of speech, the world of vitality, and the world of nature. The first part of the Tabernacle, within the veil, is where the Ark, the Tablets, and the Cherubim are — these are inward, concealed vessels, and they are the Chariot for the Name, may He be exalted, as it is written (Ezekiel 10:19): ״וכבוד אלהי ישראל עליהם מלמעלה״, and it is written (Psalms 80:2): יושב הכרובים — and this corresponds to the world of angels, which are separate, concealed forms, and they are separate intelligences, a Chariot and Throne for the Holy One, blessed be He. Their counterpart in man is the world of speech, and that is the head, which is the dwelling-place of wisdom, and there the intellect flows upon the brain, for the perfect righteous one is a Chariot for the Holy One, blessed be He, just as the fathers of the world were, and the Shekhinah dwells upon him through the commandment of tefillin on his head and his arm, corresponding to the brain and the heart, which correspond to the two Cherubim. From here you can contemplate the likeness of one who lays tefillin, and how great is his power. The second part, outside the veil, is the Tent of Meeting, and there are the Table, the Menorah, and the Incense Altar — these are honored, inward vessels, not of the preeminence of the first inmost ones, and they correspond to the world of the spheres, which are honored, numbered existences, the glory of El, and the world is sustained through their motion. Their counterpart in man is the world of vitality, and that is the heart, which is among the inward organs upon which all vitality depends, and through whose motion the entire body is sustained. The third part, the court of the Tabernacle, contained the bronze altar, which is the altar of burnt offering upon which the sacrifices are offered, and there animals suffer loss; and it corresponds to this lowly world, which is subject to coming-into-being and perishing. Their counterpart in man is the world of nature, and that is from the navel downward, for from there comes coming-into-being, and it is the beginning of the body's perishing, for perishing is the cause of coming-into-being: were there no perishing in the world, coming-into-being would not be required. From this you have learned that the Tabernacle is in three parts corresponding to the three parts of existence, concerning which David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 103:20–22): ״ברכו ה' מלאכיו גבורי כח … ברכו ה' כל צבאיו … ברכו ה' כל מעשיו״. And so too Solomon his son took this path and alluded to it in three consecutive verses: the first (Song of Songs 5:14): ״לחייו כערוגת הבשם״, the second (ibid.): ״ידיו גלילי זהב״, the third (ibid.): ״שוקיו עמודי שש״. And I have already explained the matter of the ladder." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״את תבנית המשכן ואת תבנית כל כליו״ — this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses green fire, black fire, white fire, red fire. He said to him: 'According to all that I show you, etc.' Moses said to him, etc. A parable: to what may the matter be compared? To a king who has a garment of pearls. He said to one of his servants: 'Make me one like this.' He said to him: 'My lord the king, but from where do I have a garment of pearls?' So Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'Master of the universe, from where do I have green fire, black fire, white fire, red fire?' He said to him: 'You, with your spices; and I, with My glory.' And not only that, but if you make the upper thing below, I leave My sanqliton and descend and contract My Shekhinah among you below. Above, Seraphim stand; so below, boards of shittim wood stand. Above, stars; so below, stars. Rabbi Chiya said: this teaches that the golden clasps appeared in the Tabernacle like the stars that appear in the firmament."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:15.184782037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}